{
 "_source": "Hand-curated from the Wikipedia tournament pages for each World Baseball Classic (the infobox '| champion =' and '| second =' fields name the two finalists; the roster sections name the players). Curated the same way data/owners.json is, and read the same way: the pipeline consumes this file, it never fetches it. Baseball-Reference is not touched for any of it.",
 "_ids": "brId is the Baseball-Reference player id, the key every card joins on. Names are as Wikipedia spells them, which is not always how B-R does: Wikipedia writes Korean names family-name-first (Choo Shin-soo is B-R's Shin-Soo Choo, choosh01). Where two B-R ids share a name, the note field records how the ambiguity was settled.",
 "_unresolved": "A null brId means the player has no Baseball-Reference id to join on. Almost all of them are NPB, KBO or Cuban national-team players who never played an MLB season, so they can never appear on a card and award nothing. They stay in the file on purpose: the roster is the record of what was checked, and a missing name reads as an oversight where a null brId reads as a decision.",
 "_placing": "champion and runnerUp are the placings; the point values live in pipeline/scoring.py (WBC_CHAMPION_POINTS, WBC_RUNNERUP_POINTS) so the scorer and the data can never disagree about what a gold medal is worth.",
